.TH "PAIRDESK" "1" "November 2026" "PairDesk 2.9.2" "User Commands"
.hy
.SH NAME
.PP
pairdesk - use another computer\[cq]s desktop with your USB devices,
folders and sound
.SH SYNOPSIS
.PP
\f[B]pairdesk\f[R] [\f[I]COMMAND\f[R] [\f[I]ARGUMENTS\f[R]]]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
Without a command, \f[B]pairdesk\f[R] opens the app.
PairDesk connects Linux computers on the same network: full remote
desktop with clipboard, sound and microphone, USB devices (security
keys, webcams, drives) in both directions, and shared folders in both
directions.
.PP
The same package goes on every computer.
A computer \f[I]accepts connections\f[R] only while remote access is on,
and only from computers paired with a one-time code.
Connections use PairDesk\[cq]s own encrypted link on its own port
(default 4722).
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
\f[B]access on\f[R]|\f[B]off\f[R]
Let paired computers connect, or stop that and close PairDesk\[cq]s
port.
.TP
\f[B]pairing\f[R]
On a computer without a screen (over SSH): show the code a pairing
request is waiting for, or make a new one, explain what to type on the
other computer, and wait until it is used.
.TP
\f[B]pair\f[R] \f[I]ADDRESS\f[R] [\f[I]CODE\f[R]]
Pair with a remote computer.
\f[I]ADDRESS\f[R] can be just its IP (\f[B]192.168.1.20\f[R]), a
computer name (\f[B]office-pc.local\f[R]), or
\f[B]user\[at]host:port\f[R]; the user name and port are filled in from
the remote computer.
Without \f[I]CODE\f[R] it asks the remote computer to show one, then
asks you to type it.
.TP
\f[B]pair\f[R] \f[I]ADDRESS\f[R] \f[B]\[en]ssh\f[R] [\f[I]LOGIN\f[R]]
Pair by signing in to the remote computer over SSH (its password;
\f[I]LOGIN\f[R] defaults to the user and address of the remote
computer): PairDesk gets a one-time code there and pairs with it, with
no code to type.
Works for a computer without a screen, or one that accepts pairing only
after \[lq]Pair a new computer\[rq].
.TP
\f[B]pair-code\f[R] [\f[B]\[en]plain\f[R]] [\f[B]\[en]turn-on\f[R]]
Show a one-time code so another computer can pair.
\f[B]\[en]plain\f[R] prints only the code, and \f[B]\[en]turn-on\f[R]
turns remote access on first (used by \[lq]Pair over SSH\[rq]).
.TP
\f[B]headless-setup\f[R] [\f[B]\[en]from-client\f[R] \f[I]NAME\f[R]]
Guided setup of a computer without a monitor, run on that computer over
SSH.
Each step is checked first and you are asked before anything changes.
\[lq]Set it up over SSH\[rq] in the app runs it with
\f[B]\[en]from-client\f[R] after installing or updating PairDesk there
if needed.
.TP
\f[B]doctor\f[R]
Check the current computer and say how to fix anything missing.
Changes nothing.
Exits with 1 when something needs fixing.
.TP
\f[B]list\f[R]
Paired computers, in both directions.
.TP
\f[B]unpair\f[R] \f[I]NAME\f[R]
Remove a pairing on both computers.
.TP
\f[B]update\f[R] \f[I]NAME\f[R]
Compare PairDesk on this computer and \f[I]NAME\f[R] and, after asking,
update whichever is older: copy the newer package from \f[I]NAME\f[R]
and install it here (sudo asks for your password), or send this
computer\[cq]s package to \f[I]NAME\f[R], where an administrator must
approve it.
.TP
\f[B]update \[en]install\f[R]
Install the update a paired computer sent here (e.g.\ over SSH on a
computer without a screen).
.TP
\f[B]desktop gnome\f[R]|\f[B]xrdp\f[R]
Choose the desktop server.
GNOME on Wayland only (GNOME 49 and later, Ubuntu 25.10 and later) needs
GNOME Remote Login, because xrdp can\[cq]t start a desktop there.
Asks for the Remote Login user name and password.
.TP
\f[B]connect\f[R] \f[I]NAME\f[R] [\f[B]\[en]no-desktop\f[R]]
Connect from the terminal.
.TP
\f[B]usb\f[R]
USB devices on the current computer.
.TP
\f[B]send\f[R] \f[I]NAME\f[R] \f[I]KEY\f[R]|\f[B]off\f[R], \f[B]take\f[R] \f[I]NAME\f[R] \f[I]KEY\f[R]|\f[B]off\f[R]
Choose a USB device to send to \f[I]NAME\f[R], or one of
\f[I]NAME\f[R]\[cq]s devices to use here, on connect.
.TP
\f[B]port\f[R] \f[I]N\f[R]
Use port \f[I]N\f[R] for PairDesk\[cq]s link.
.TP
\f[B]scan\f[R]
Find computers on the local network that accept connections (no add-on
needed).
.TP
\f[B]setup\f[R], \f[B]unsetup\f[R]
Optional: set up or undo USB sharing and the network rule (asks for the
admin password).
.TP
\f[B]features\f[R], \f[B]install\f[R] \f[I]NAME\f[R]
Show the optional add-ons, or install one.
.SH FILES
.TP
\f[I]\[ti]/.config/pairdesk/\f[R]
Settings, keys and the link service\[cq]s configuration.
.TP
\f[I]\[ti]/PairDesk Shared/\f[R]
The folder shared with paired computers (can be changed in Settings).
.TP
\f[I]\[ti]/PairDesk/NAME/\f[R]
Where a remote computer\[cq]s shared folder appears.
.TP
\f[I]\[ti]/.cache/pairdesk/updates/\f[R]
A PairDesk package a paired computer sent, until it\[cq]s installed.
.SH SEE ALSO
.PP
\f[B]pairdesk-uninstall\f[R](8), \f[B]xfreerdp\f[R](1),
\f[B]usbip\f[R](8)
.PP
Website: https://pairdesk.knowreva.com
.SH AUTHOR
.PP
KnowReva.
